METHOD NOTE — mutation testing without writing to the shared tree
The coordinator proposed cp-backup + sed + mv-restore. I used Go's build
overlay instead (`go test -overlay=<json>`), which substitutes file contents
in the BUILD ONLY and never writes the working tree. In a tree with a live
concurrent run this is strictly safer: there is no window in which a mutated
sync.go exists on disk for another agent to copy or compile. The restore
guarantee the coordinator asked for is therefore vacuous by construction, but
I verified it anyway with sha256sum bracketing every mutation run:
  BEFORE all mutations:
    30a0f0919819c89f549b00b951608f9dee59fc1cd473560aeae007a9ce583a9b  internal/handlers/whatif/sync.go
    fe7788797cb94107e188934e3a35853effa8ff39f8f83804f68a422a2a44fe09  internal/handlers/whatif/sync_plan_exclusions_test.go
  AFTER all mutations:
    30a0f0919819c89f549b00b951608f9dee59fc1cd473560aeae007a9ce583a9b  internal/handlers/whatif/sync.go
    fe7788797cb94107e188934e3a35853effa8ff39f8f83804f68a422a2a44fe09  internal/handlers/whatif/sync_plan_exclusions_test.go
  Byte-identical. My only write to the tree is this verdict file.
Ports: I started nothing myself; the oracle binds 18094 as designed. I never
touched 18093/18095-18098 (HC) or 8080 (live app), and never executed the
prebuilt ./budget2 or ./server binaries. No write-mode git.

